    "\$f'(x) = 2 e^{2x}\$ by the chain rule. Differentiating again, \$f''(x) = 2 \\cdot 2 e^{2x} = 4 e^{2x}\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 2 x - 6\$. Setting \$f'(x) = 0\$: \$2 x - 6 = 0\$, which gives \$x = 3\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 2x - 4 = 0\$ at \$x=2\$, an interior critical point. Checking \$f\$ at the critical point and both endpoints: \$f(0)=3\$, \$f(2) = 4-8+3=-1\$, \$f(5) = 25-20+3=8\$. The smallest of these is \$f(2) = -1\$, so the absolute minimum on \$[0,5]\$ is \$-1\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3 \\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x + 1\\right)\$. Solving \$f'(x) < 0\$ shows \$f'(x)\$ is negative for \$x \\in \\left(-1, 1\\right)\$, so \$f\$ is decreasing on \$\\left(-1, 1\\right)\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3 x^{2} - 12\$. Setting \$f'(x) = 0\$: \$3 x^{2} - 12 = 0\$, which gives \$x = -2, 2\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3 x^{2} - 3\$. Setting \$f'(x) = 0\$: \$3 x^{2} - 3 = 0\$, which gives \$x = -1, 1\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2} - 2\$, so \$f''(x) = 6x\$. At \$x=1\$: \$f''(1) = 6(1) = 6\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2} - 3 = 3(x-1)(x+1)\$, so the critical points are \$x=-1\$ and \$x=1\$. For \$x\$ slightly less than \$-1\$, \$f'(x) > 0\$ (increasing); for \$x\$ slightly greater than \$-1\$, \$f'(x) < 0\$ (decreasing). Since \$f'\$ changes from positive to negative, \$x=-1\$ is a relative maximum.",
    "\$f'(x) = 3x^{2} - 6x = 3x(x-2)\$, so \$x=0\$ and \$x=2\$ are critical points. \$f''(x) = 6x - 6\$, so \$f''(2) = 6(2) - 6 = 6 > 0\$, meaning \$x=2\$ is a relative minimum by the second derivative test.",
    "\$f'(x) = 4x^{3} - 6x\$. Differentiating again, \$f''(x) = 12x^{2} - 6\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 4x^{3} - 8x = 4x(x^{2}-2)\$, so the critical points are \$x=0, \\sqrt{2}, -\\sqrt{2}\$. \$f''(x) = 12x^{2} - 8\$, so \$f''(0) = -8 < 0\$. Since \$f''(0) < 0\$, the second derivative test says \$x=0\$ is a relative maximum.",
    "\$f'(x) = 6 x^{2} - 6 x - 12\$. Setting \$f'(x) = 0\$: \$6 x^{2} - 6 x - 12 = 0\$, which gives \$x = -1, 2\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = 6x^{2} - 6x = 6x(x-1)\$, so \$x=1\$ is a critical point. \$f''(x) = 12x - 6\$, so \$f''(1) = 12(1) - 6 = 6 > 0\$. Since \$f''(1) > 0\$, the second derivative test says \$x=1\$ is a relative minimum.",
    "\$f'(x) = \\cos(x)\$, \$f''(x) = -\\sin(x)\$, and differentiating once more, \$f'''(x) = -\\cos(x)\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\$. Differentiating \$x^{-1}\$ again gives \$f''(x) = -x^{-2} = -\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = \\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\ln(u)] = \\dfrac{u'}{u}\$ with \$u = x^{2} + 1\$: \$f'(x) = \\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + 1}\$. At \$x=2\$: \$f'(2) = \\frac{2 \\cdot 2}{2^{2} + 1} = \\frac{4}{5}\$.",
    "\$f'(x) = \\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^u] = e^u \\cdot u'\$ with \$u = 3 x\$: \$f'(x) = 3 e^{3 x}\$. At \$x=0\$: \$f'(0) = 3 e^{3 \\cdot 0} = 3\$.",
    "\$f(-1) = 1\$, \$f'(-1) = -2\$. Using the point-slope form \$y - f(a) = f'(a)(x-a)\$: \$y - 1 = -2(x - -1)\$, which simplifies to \$y = -2x - 1\$.",
    "\$f(1) = 0\$, \$f'(1) = -1\$. Using the point-slope form \$y - f(a) = f'(a)(x-a)\$: \$y - 0 = -1(x - 1)\$, which simplifies to \$y = -1x + 1\$.",
    "\$f(1) = 1\$, \$f'(1) = 3\$. Using the point-slope form \$y - f(a) = f'(a)(x-a)\$: \$y - 1 = 3(x - 1)\$, which simplifies to \$y = 3x - 2\$.",
    "\$f(2)\$ is undefined (division by zero), but \$\\lim_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{x^{2}-4}{x-2} = \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2} = \\lim_{x \\to 2}(x+2) = 4\$, which exists. Since the limit exists even though \$f(2)\$ does not, the discontinuity can be removed by defining \$f(2)=4\$ -- this is a removable discontinuity.",
    "\$f(3) = 9\$, \$f'(3) = 6\$. Using the point-slope form \$y - f(a) = f'(a)(x-a)\$: \$y - 9 = 6(x - 3)\$, which simplifies to \$y = 6x - 9\$.",
    "\$f(x) = \\sqrt{x-1}\$ requires \$x-1 \\geq 0\$, i.e. \$x \\geq 1\$. \$g(x) = \\sqrt{3-x}\$ requires \$3-x \\geq 0\$, i.e. \$x \\leq 3\$. The domain of the sum is the intersection of both: \$1 \\leq x \\leq 3\$.",
    "\$f(x+h) - f(x) = (x+h)^{2} + 2(x+h) - x^{2} - 2x = 2xh + h^{2} + 2h\$, so \$\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} = 2x + h + 2\$. Letting \$h \\to 0\$ gives \$f'(x) = 2x + 2\$, so \$f'(1) = 2(1) + 2 = 4\$.",
    "\$f(x+h) - f(x) = (x+h)^{2} - 3(x+h) - x^{2} + 3x = 2xh + h^{2} - 3h\$, so \$\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} = 2x + h - 3\$. Letting \$h \\to 0\$ gives \$f'(x) = 2x - 3\$, so \$f'(2) = 2(2) - 3 = 1\$.",
    "\$f(x+h) = 3(x+h) + 2 = 3x + 3h + 2\$, so \$\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} = \\dfrac{3h}{h} = 3\$ for every \$h \\neq 0\$. Letting \$h \\to 0\$, \$f'(x) = 3\$ -- a straight line's slope never changes.",
