    "\$y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\$, so the tangent's slope at \$(2,\\tfrac{1}{2})\$ is \$y'(2) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\$. The normal's slope is the negative reciprocal, \$4\$. Point-slope form gives \$y - \\dfrac{1}{2} = 4(x-2)\$, which simplifies to \$y = 4x - \\dfrac{15}{2}\$.",
    "\$y' = 2x - 4\$, so at \$x=3\$ the slope is \$y'(3) = 2(3) - 4 = 2\$.",
    "\$y' = 3x^{2}\$, so the slope of the tangent at \$(-1,0)\$ is \$y'(-1) = 3\$. The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is \$-\\dfrac{1}{3}\$. Using point-slope form: \$y - 0 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x - (-1))\$, i.e. \$3y = -(x+1)\$, which rearranges to \$x + 3y = -1\$.",
    "\$y' = 6x\$, so the slope at \$(1,4)\$ is \$y'(1) = 6\$. Using the point-slope form \$y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)\$ with \$m=6\$, \$(x_0,y_0)=(1,4)\$: \$y - 4 = 6(x - 1)\$, which simplifies to \$y = 6x - 2\$.",
    "\$y(2) = 200 e^{2k} = 800\$, so \$e^{2k} = 4\$. Taking the natural log: \$2k = \\ln 4\$, so \$k = \\dfrac{\\ln 4}{2}\$.",
    "\$y(2) = y_0 e^{2k} = 3y_0\$, so \$e^{2k}=3\$. Taking the natural log of both sides: \$2k = \\ln 3\$, so \$k = \\dfrac{\\ln 3}{2}\$.",
    "\$y=3 x\$ does not cross the \$x\$-axis between \$x=0\$ and \$x=4\$, so the area is \$\\int_{0}^{4} 3 x \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{3 x^{2}}{2}\$, so the area is \$F(4) - F(0) = \\frac{3 \\cdot 4^{2}}{2} - (\\frac{3 \\cdot 0^{2}}{2}) = 24\$.",
    "\$y=4 - x^{2}\$ does not cross the \$x\$-axis between \$x=-2\$ and \$x=2\$, so the area is \$\\int_{-2}^{2} 4 - x^{2} \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 4 x\$, so the area is \$F(2) - F(-2) = \\frac{2 \\left(12 - 2^{2}\\right)}{3} - (\\frac{\\left(-2\\right) \\left(12 - \\left(-2\\right)^{2}\\right)}{3}) = \\frac{32}{3}\$.",
    "\$y=x^{2}\$ does not cross the \$x\$-axis between \$x=0\$ and \$x=2\$, so the area is \$\\int_{0}^{2} x^{2} \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{x^{3}}{3}\$, so the area is \$F(2) - F(0) = \\frac{2^{3}}{3} - (\\frac{0^{3}}{3}) = \\frac{8}{3}\$.",
    "\$y=x^{3}\$ does not cross the \$x\$-axis between \$x=0\$ and \$x=2\$, so the area is \$\\int_{0}^{2} x^{3} \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{x^{4}}{4}\$, so the area is \$F(2) - F(0) = \\frac{2^{4}}{4} - (\\frac{0^{4}}{4}) = 4\$.",
