    "A ball thrown upward has height s(t) = 16t - t^2 (metres, t in seconds). At what time does the ball reach its maximum height (v = 0)?",
    "A curve \$y = f(x)\$ is symmetric about the origin if f(-x) = -f(x) (an odd function). Determine the symmetry of \$y = x^3 - x\$.",
    "A curve is symmetric about the origin if replacing both x by -x and y by -y leaves its equation unchanged. Determine the symmetry of xy = 4.",
    "A curve is symmetric about the y-axis if replacing x by -x leaves its equation unchanged. Determine the symmetry of \$y = x^4 - 3x^2 + 1\$.",
    "A function f is defined by \$f(x) = x+1\$ for \$x<1\$ and \$f(x) = 4-x\$ for x greater than or equal to 1. Classify the discontinuity of f at x = 1.",
    "A function f is defined by \$f(x) = x+1\$ for \$x<1\$ and \$f(x) = 4-x\$ for x greater than or equal to 1. Find \$\\lim_{x \\to 1^+} [f(x)]\$.",
    "A function f is defined by \$f(x) = x+1\$ for \$x<1\$ and \$f(x) = 4-x\$ for x greater than or equal to 1. Find \$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} [f(x)]\$.",
    "A population grows exponentially and doubles every 3 hours. If it starts at 100, how many are there after 9 hours?",
    "A quantity grows according to y(t) = y0 * e^(kt). If the quantity triples in 2 years, find k (in terms of ln, per year).",
    "A radioactive substance has a half-life of 30 years. If 100 mg is present initially, approximately how much remains after 100 years?",
    "A vertical stretch by factor \$3\$ multiplies the output: \$3f(x)\$. Shifting the graph 2 units to the left replaces \$x\$ with \$x+2\$ inside: \$y = 3f(x+2)\$.",
    "Acceleration is \$a(t) = v'(t) = 6t - 6\$. At \$t=2\$: \$a(2) = 6(2) - 6 = 6\$.",
    "Acceleration is \$a(t) = v'(t) = s''(t) = 6t - 12\$. At \$t=1\$: \$a(1) = 6(1) - 12 = -6\$.",
    "An antiderivative is \$F(x) = - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 4 x\$. By the Fundamental Theorem, \$\\int_{-1}^{1} 4 - x^{2} \\, dx = F(1) - F(-1) = (\\frac{1 \\left(12 - 1^{2}\\right)}{3} = \\frac{11}{3}) - (\\frac{\\left(-1\\right) \\left(12 - \\left(-1\\right)^{2}\\right)}{3} = - \\frac{11}{3}) = \\frac{11}{3} - - \\frac{11}{3} = \\frac{22}{3}\$.",
    "An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{3 x^{2}}{2} - x\$. By the Fundamental Theorem, \$\\int_{1}^{4} 3 x - 1 \\, dx = F(4) - F(1) = (\\frac{4 \\left(-2 + 3 \\cdot 4\\right)}{2} = 20) - (\\frac{1 \\left(-2 + 3 \\cdot 1\\right)}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}) = 20 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{39}{2}\$.",
    "An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{x^{3}}{3} - 2 x^{2} + 3 x\$. By the Fundamental Theorem, \$\\int_{0}^{3} x^{2} - 4 x + 3 \\, dx = F(3) - F(0) = (\\frac{3 \\left(9 - 6 \\cdot 3 + 3^{2}\\right)}{3} = 0) - (\\frac{0 \\left(9 - 6 \\cdot 0 + 0^{2}\\right)}{3} = 0) = 0 - 0 = 0\$.",
    "An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{x^{3}}{3}\$. By the Fundamental Theorem, \$\\int_{0}^{2} x^{2} \\, dx = F(2) - F(0) = (\\frac{2^{3}}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}) - (\\frac{0^{3}}{3} = 0) = \\frac{8}{3} - 0 = \\frac{8}{3}\$.",
    "An antiderivative is \$F(x) = \\frac{x^{4}}{4}\$. By the Fundamental Theorem, \$\\int_{0}^{1} x^{3} \\, dx = F(1) - F(0) = (\\frac{1^{4}}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}) - (\\frac{0^{4}}{4} = 0) = \\frac{1}{4} - 0 = \\frac{1}{4}\$.",
    "An antiderivative is \$F(x) = x^{2} + x\$. By the Fundamental Theorem, \$\\int_{-1}^{2} 2 x + 1 \\, dx = F(2) - F(-1) = (2^{2} + 2 = 6) - (\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + -1 = 0) = 6 - 0 = 6\$.",
    "An object's position is s(t) = t^2 - 4t + 3. At what time t is the object momentarily at rest (v = 0)?",
    "Approaching \$x=2\$ from above, \$x \\geq 2\$ applies, so \$f(x) = x^{2} - 1\$ is the relevant branch. The right-hand limit is \$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = 2^{2} - 1 = 3\$.",
    "Approaching \$x=2\$ from below, \$x < 2\$ applies, so \$f(x) = 3 - x\$ is the relevant branch. The left-hand limit is \$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = 3 - 2 = 1\$.",
    "As \$x \\to 0\$ from either side, \$x^{2} \\to 0^{+}\$ (always positive), so \$\\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}\$ grows without bound. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1}{x^{2}} = \\infty\$.",
    "As \$x \\to 0\$, \$x^{4} \\to 0^{+}\$, so \$\\dfrac{1}{x^{4}} \\to \\infty\$ and therefore \$-\\dfrac{1}{x^{4}} \\to -\\infty\$. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{x^{4}}\\right) = -\\infty\$.",
    "As \$x \\to 0^{+}\$, \$\\dfrac{1}{x} \\to \\infty\$, and as \$x \\to 0^{-}\$, \$\\dfrac{1}{x} \\to -\\infty\$. Neither one-sided limit exists as a finite number, so this cannot be patched by redefining a single point -- it is an essential (infinite) discontinuity.",
    "As \$x \\to 2^{+}\$, \$x-2 \\to 0^{+}\$ (a small positive number), so \$\\dfrac{1}{x-2}\$ grows without bound through positive values. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 2^+} \\dfrac{1}{x-2} = \\infty\$.",
    "As \$x \\to 2^{-}\$, \$x-2 \\to 0^{-}\$ (a small negative number), so \$\\dfrac{1}{x-2}\$ decreases without bound through negative values. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac{1}{x-2} = -\\infty\$.",
    "As \$x \\to 3\$ from either side, \$(x-3)^{2} \\to 0^{+}\$ (a square is never negative), so \$\\dfrac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\$ grows without bound through positive values. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 3} \\dfrac{1}{(x-3)^{2}} = \\infty\$.",
    "At \$x=3\$ and just above it, on the interval \$[3, 4)\$, the greatest integer function \$[x]\$ equals \$3\$. So the right-hand limit is \$\\lim_{x \\to 3^+} [x] = 3\$.",
    "At the critical point x = -1 of \$f(x) = x^3 - 3x\$, the Second Derivative Test shows this is a:",
    "At the critical point x = 1 of \$f(x) = x^3 - 3x\$, the Second Derivative Test shows this is a:",
    "At the critical point x = 2 of \$f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 7\$, the Second Derivative Test shows this is a:",
    "At the critical point x = 3 of \$f(x) = -x^2 + 6x - 5\$, the Second Derivative Test shows this is a:",
    "Bacteria in a culture grow exponentially. If the population is 200 at t=0 and 800 at t=2 (hours), find the growth constant k in y(t) = 200 e^(kt).",
    "Between \$x=-1\$ and \$x=1\$, \$y=x + 3\$ lies above \$y=x^{2} - 1\$, so the area is \$\\int_{-1}^{1} [(x + 3) - (x^{2} - 1)] \\, dx = \\int_{-1}^{1} - x^{2} + x + 4 \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 4 x\$, so the area is \$F(1) - F(-1) = \\frac{1 \\left(24 + 3 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot 1^{2}\\right)}{6} - (\\frac{\\left(-1\\right) \\left(24 + 3 \\left(-1\\right) - 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2}\\right)}{6}) = \\frac{22}{3}\$.",
    "Between \$x=-2\$ and \$x=3\$, \$y=x + 5\$ lies above \$y=x^{2} - 1\$, so the area is \$\\int_{-2}^{3} [(x + 5) - (x^{2} - 1)] \\, dx = \\int_{-2}^{3} - x^{2} + x + 6 \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 6 x\$, so the area is \$F(3) - F(-2) = \\frac{3 \\left(36 + 3 \\cdot 3 - 2 \\cdot 3^{2}\\right)}{6} - (\\frac{\\left(-2\\right) \\left(36 + 3 \\left(-2\\right) - 2 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}\\right)}{6}) = \\frac{125}{6}\$.",
    "Between \$x=-3\$ and \$x=2\$, \$y=6 - x\$ lies above \$y=x^{2}\$, so the area is \$\\int_{-3}^{2} [(6 - x) - (x^{2})] \\, dx = \\int_{-3}^{2} - x^{2} - x + 6 \\, dx\$. An antiderivative is \$F(x) = - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 6 x\$, so the area is \$F(2) - F(-3) = \\frac{2 \\left(36 - 3 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 2^{2}\\right)}{6} - (\\frac{\\left(-3\\right) \\left(36 - 3 \\left(-3\\right) - 2 \\left(-3\\right)^{2}\\right)}{6}) = \\frac{125}{6}\$.",
    "By definition, \$f'(x) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\$. Here \$f(x+h) = (x+h)^2 = x^2 + 2xh + h^2\$, so \$\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} = \\dfrac{2xh + h^2}{h} = 2x + h\$. Letting \$h \\to 0\$ gives \$f'(x) = 2x\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\ln(u)] = \\dfrac{u'}{u}\$. With \$u = 3 x^{2} + 5\$, \$u' = 6 x\$, giving \$f'(x) = \\dfrac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 5} = \\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 5}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\ln(u)] = \\dfrac{u'}{u}\$. With \$u = 5 x - 1\$, \$u' = 5\$, giving \$f'(x) = \\dfrac{5}{5 x - 1} = \\frac{5}{5 x - 1}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\ln(u)] = \\dfrac{u'}{u}\$. With \$u = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$, \$u' = - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$, giving \$f'(x) = \\dfrac{- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}} = - \\tan{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\ln(u)] = \\dfrac{u'}{u}\$. With \$u = x^{3} - 2\$, \$u' = 3 x^{2}\$, giving \$f'(x) = \\dfrac{3 x^{2}}{x^{3} - 2} = \\frac{3 x^{2}}{x^{3} - 2}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^u] = e^u \\cdot u'\$. With \$u = 2 x - 1\$, \$u' = 2\$, giving \$f'(x) = e^{2 x - 1} \\cdot 2 = 2 e^{2 x - 1}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^u] = e^u \\cdot u'\$. With \$u = 4 x\$, \$u' = 4\$, giving \$f'(x) = e^{4 x} \\cdot 4 = 4 e^{4 x}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^u] = e^u \\cdot u'\$. With \$u = \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$, \$u' = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$, giving \$f'(x) = e^{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}} \\cdot \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} = e^{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "By the Chain Rule, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[e^u] = e^u \\cdot u'\$. With \$u = x^{2}\$, \$u' = 2 x\$, giving \$f'(x) = e^{x^{2}} \\cdot 2 x = 2 x e^{x^{2}}\$.",
    "By the shell method, revolving about the y-axis gives \$V = 2\\pi \\int_{0}^{2} x \\cdot f(x) \\, dx = 2\\pi \\int_{0}^{2} x \\cdot x^{2} \\, dx = 2\\pi \\int_{0}^{2} x^{3} \\, dx\$. An antiderivative of the integrand is \$F(x) = \\dfrac{x^{4}}{4}\$, so \$V = 2\\pi \\left[F(2)-F(0)\\right] = 2\\pi \\left[\\dfrac{16}{4} - 0\\right] = 2\\pi(4) = 8\\pi\$.",
    "Chain Rule: with \$u = 2 x + 3\$, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(u)] = \\cos(u) \\cdot u'\$. Here \$u' = 2\$, so \$f'(x) = \\cos(2 x + 3) \\cdot (2) = 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x + 3 \\right)}\$.",
    "Chain Rule: with \$u = 2 x - 5\$, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u^{4}] = 4 u^{3} \\cdot u'\$. Here \$u' = 2\$, so \$f'(x) = 4(2 x - 5)^{3} \\cdot (2) = 64 x^{3} - 480 x^{2} + 1200 x - 1000\$.",
    "Chain Rule: with \$u = 3 x - 1\$, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(u)] = \\cos(u) \\cdot u'\$. Here \$u' = 3\$, so \$f'(x) = \\cos(3 x - 1) \\cdot (3) = 3 \\cos{\\left(3 x - 1 \\right)}\$.",
    "Chain Rule: with \$u = x^{2} + 1\$, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u^{3}] = 3 u^{2} \\cdot u'\$. Here \$u' = 2 x\$, so \$f'(x) = 3(x^{2} + 1)^{2} \\cdot (2 x) = 6 x^{5} + 12 x^{3} + 6 x\$.",
    "Chain Rule: with \$u = x^{2}\$, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(u)] = \\cos(u) \\cdot u'\$. Here \$u' = 2 x\$, so \$f'(x) = \\cos(x^{2}) \\cdot (2 x) = 2 x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} \\right)}\$.",
    "Chain Rule: with \$u = x^{3} - 2 x\$, \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[u^{2}] = 2 u^{1} \\cdot u'\$. Here \$u' = 3 x^{2} - 2\$, so \$f'(x) = 2(x^{3} - 2 x)^{1} \\cdot (3 x^{2} - 2) = 6 x^{5} - 16 x^{3} + 8 x\$.",
    "Classify the discontinuity of \$f(x) = 1/x\$ at x = 0.",
    "Clearing denominators: \$1 = A(x+1) + B(x-1)\$. Setting \$x=-1\$: \$1 = A(0) + B(-2) \\Rightarrow B = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\$.",
    "Clearing denominators: \$1 = A(x+1) + B(x-1)\$. Setting \$x=1\$: \$1 = A(2) + B(0) \\Rightarrow A = \\dfrac{1}{2}\$.",
    "Clearing denominators: \$1 = A(x-1)^{2} + Bx(x-1) + Cx\$. Setting \$x=1\$ eliminates the \$A\$ and \$B\$ terms: \$1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(1) \\Rightarrow C = 1\$.",
    "Clearing denominators: \$3x+1 = A(x+2) + B(x-1)\$. Setting \$x=1\$: \$3(1)+1 = A(3) \\Rightarrow 4 = 3A \\Rightarrow A = \\dfrac{4}{3}\$.",
    "Clearing denominators: \$5 = A(x+3) + B(x-2)\$. Setting \$x=2\$: \$5 = A(5) \\Rightarrow A=1\$. Setting \$x=-3\$: \$5 = B(-5) \\Rightarrow B=-1\$.",
    "Differentiate both sides of \$x^{2} + y^{2} = 25\$ implicitly with respect to \$x\$, treating \$y\$ as a function of \$x\$: \$2 x + 2 y(dy/dx) = 0\$. Solving for \$dy/dx\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{x}{y}\$. At \$(3,4)\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{3}{4} = - \\frac{3}{4}\$.",
    "Differentiate both sides of \$x^{3} + y^{3} = 9\$ implicitly with respect to \$x\$, treating \$y\$ as a function of \$x\$: \$3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}(dy/dx) = 0\$. Solving for \$dy/dx\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}\$. At \$(1,2)\$: \$dy/dx = - \\frac{1}{4} = - \\frac{1}{4}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[2 \\cdot x^{7}] = 2 \\cdot 7 x^{6} = 14 x^{6}\$; the derivative of the constant \$5\$ is \$0\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 14 x^{6}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[3 \\cdot \\sin(x)] = 3 \\cdot \\cos(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[3 \\cdot x^{4}] = 3 \\cdot 4 x^{3} = 12 x^{3}\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 12 x^{3}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[7 \\cdot x^{3}] = 7 \\cdot 3 x^{2} = 21 x^{2}\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[-2 \\cdot x^{2}] = -2 \\cdot 2 x^{1} = - 4 x\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{1}] = 1 x^{0} = 1\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 21 x^{2} - 4 x + 1\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\cos(x)] = -\\sin(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{2}] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 x^{1} = x\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[-3 \\cdot x^{1}] = -3 \\cdot 1 x^{0} = -3\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = x - 3\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(x)] = \\cos(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\sin(x)] = \\cos(x)\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\cos(x)] = -\\sin(x)\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}] = \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 1 + \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{5}] = 5 x^{4} = 5 x^{4}\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 5 x^{4}\$.",
    "Differentiate term by term: \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[x^{6}] = 6 x^{5} = 6 x^{5}\$; \$\\dfrac{d}{dx}[-4 \\cdot x^{1}] = -4 \\cdot 1 x^{0} = -4\$. Summing the terms: \$f'(x) = 6 x^{5} - 4\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x + 3\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 3\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2 + 3 = 5\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 1}{x - 1} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x + 1\\right)}{x - 1}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 1\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 1\$). Substituting \$x=1\$: \$1 + 1 = 2\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 4}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 2\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2 + 2 = 4\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 5 x + 6}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(x - 2\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x - 3\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2 - 3 = -1\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} - 9}{x - 3} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(x + 3\\right)}{x - 3}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x + 3\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 3\$). Substituting \$x=3\$: \$3 + 3 = 6\$.",
    "Direct substitution gives \$\\frac{0}{0}\$, a \$0/0\$ form. Factor: \$\\dfrac{x^{3} - 8}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x^{2} + 2 x + 4\\right)}{x - 2}\$, and cancel the common factor to get \$x^{2} + 2 x + 4\$ (valid for \$x \\neq 2\$). Substituting \$x=2\$: \$2^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2 + 4 = 12\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{1}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{3 x + 1}{6 x + 5} = \\dfrac{3 + \\frac{1}{x}}{6 + \\frac{5}{x}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{3 x + 1}{6 x + 5} = \\frac{1}{2}\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{2}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x + 3}{x^{2} - 4} = \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{3}{x^{2}}}{1 - \\frac{4}{x^{2}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{x + 3}{x^{2} - 4} = 0\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{2}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x \\left(2 x + 3\\right)}{5 x^{2} - x + 1} = \\dfrac{2 + \\frac{3}{x}}{5 - \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^{2}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{2 x^{2} + 3 x}{5 x^{2} - x + 1} = \\frac{2}{5}\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{3}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x \\left(4 x^{2} - 1\\right)}{2 x^{3} + 7} = \\dfrac{4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}}{2 + \\frac{7}{x^{3}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{4 x^{3} - x}{2 x^{3} + 7} = 2\$.",
    "Divide numerator and denominator by \$x^{3}\$, the highest power of \$x\$ present: \$\\dfrac{x^{2} + 1}{x^{3} + 2} = \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}}}{1 + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}}\$. As \$x \\to \\infty\$, every term with \$x\$ in the denominator goes to \$0\$, leaving \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{x^{2} + 1}{x^{3} + 2} = 0\$.",
    "Dividing numerator and denominator by \$x\$: \$\\dfrac{3 + 1/x}{1 - 5/x} \\to \\dfrac{3}{1} = 3\$ as \$x \\to \\infty\$. So \$y=3\$ is the horizontal asymptote.",
