    "For \$(f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x))\$ to be defined, \$x\$ must first be in the domain of \$g\$: \$g(x) = \\sqrt{x-1}\$ requires \$x \\geq 1\$. Since \$f(x)=x^{2}\$ accepts any real input, no further restriction is added, so the domain of \$f \\circ g\$ is \$x \\geq 1\$.",
    "For \$(g \\circ f)(x) = g(f(x))\$, \$x\$ must first be in the domain of \$f\$: \$f(x) = \\sqrt{x}\$ requires \$x \\geq 0\$. Since \$g(x)=x-4\$ accepts any real input, no further restriction is added, so the domain of \$g \\circ f\$ is \$x \\geq 0\$.",
    "For \$f(x) = (x^2-25)/(x-5)\$ if \$x \\neq 5\$, and \$f(5)\$\$=10\$, is f continuous at x=5?",
    "For \$f(x) = (x^2-25)/(x-5)\$ if \$x \\neq 5\$, and \$f(5)\$\$=7\$, is f continuous at x=5?",
    "For \$f(x) = 2x^3 - 3x^2 + 2\$, classify the critical point at x = 1.",
    "For \$f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + 5}}\$ to be defined, the denominator must not be zero: \$\\sqrt{x + 5} \\neq 0\$, which excludes \$x = -5\$. Combining these conditions, the domain is \$\\left(-5, \\infty\\right)\$.",
    "For \$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 3}\$ to be defined, the denominator must not be zero: \$x - 3 \\neq 0\$, which excludes \$x = 3\$. Combining these conditions, the domain is \$\\left(-\\infty, 3\\right) \\cup \\left(3, \\infty\\right)\$.",
    "For \$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 4}\$ to be defined, the denominator must not be zero: \$x^{2} - 4 \\neq 0\$, which excludes \$x = -2 \\textrm{ and } x = 2\$. Combining these conditions, the domain is \$\\left(-\\infty, -2\\right) \\cup \\left(-2, 2\\right) \\cup \\left(2, \\infty\\right)\$.",
    "For \$f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{x + 1}}{x - 4}\$ to be defined, the denominator must not be zero: \$x - 4 \\neq 0\$, which excludes \$x = 4\$; and the expression under the square root must be non-negative: \$x + 1 \\geq 0\$, which gives \$\\left[-1, \\infty\\right)\$. Combining these conditions, the domain is \$\\left[-1, 4\\right) \\cup \\left(4, \\infty\\right)\$.",
    "For \$f(x) = \\sqrt{9 - x^{2}}\$ to be defined, the expression under the square root must be non-negative: \$9 - x^{2} \\geq 0\$, which gives \$\\left[-3, 3\\right]\$. Combining these conditions, the domain is \$\\left[-3, 3\\right]\$.",
    "For \$f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 2}\$ to be defined, the expression under the square root must be non-negative: \$x - 2 \\geq 0\$, which gives \$\\left[2, \\infty\\right)\$. Combining these conditions, the domain is \$\\left[2, \\infty\\right)\$.",
