    "For \$x < 0\$, \$|x| = -x\$, so \$\\dfrac{|x|}{x} = \\dfrac{-x}{x} = -1\$ for every \$x<0\$. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{|x|}{x} = -1\$.",
    "For \$x < 1\$, \$f(x) = x+1\$, so the left-hand limit uses this branch: \$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} (x+1) = 1 + 1 = 2\$.",
    "For \$x > 0\$, \$|x| = x\$, so \$\\dfrac{|x|}{x} = \\dfrac{x}{x} = 1\$ for every \$x>0\$. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 0^+} \\dfrac{|x|}{x} = 1\$.",
    "For \$x > 3\$, \$x - 3 > 0\$ so \$|x-3| = x-3\$, giving \$\\dfrac{x-3}{|x-3|} = 1\$ for every \$x\$ just above \$3\$. Hence \$\\lim_{x \\to 3^+} \\dfrac{x-3}{|x-3|} = 1\$.",
    "For \$x \\geq 1\$, \$f(x) = 4-x\$, so the right-hand limit uses this branch: \$\\lim_{x \\to 1^+} (4-x) = 4 - 1 = 3\$.",
    "For a rational function, the horizontal asymptote is \$\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{x-1}{x+2}\$. Dividing numerator and denominator by \$x\$: \$\\dfrac{1 - 1/x}{1 + 2/x} \\to \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1\$ as \$x \\to \\infty\$. So \$y=1\$ is the horizontal asymptote.",
